MIDI in and out through host devices (Windows)

An Amiga's MIDI interface hangs off the serial port: software runs the UART at 31250 baud,
which SERPER's table rounds to 31400. WinUAE sends the byte stream to a MIDI device at that
rate; this build had Midi_Open() returning 0.

- adm_hostio.c: at 31400 baud, with a device chosen, writeser() parses the byte stream
  (running status, system common lengths, real-time bytes, SysEx buffered to F7) into
  midiOutShortMsg/midiOutLongMsg, and readser() drains a ring the winmm input callback fills.
  Any other rate closes the devices and the serial target gets the bytes again. Elsewhere
  than Windows it reports that MIDI is not implemented.
- adm_midi_set_devices(out, in): out -2 none, -1 the system mapper, 0.. a device; in -1 none.
  libretro-core.c applies it on the emulation thread through the same serial close and
  re-open as a port change, mirroring it into win32_midioutdev/indev for serial_open()'s
  empty-port test, and opens the devices at once when the UART is already at MIDI speed.
- The status struct gains the devices, whether MIDI is active, and message counts.
- Makefile: -lwinmm on Windows.

Applies to libretro-uae 2b0ed42fe565fb997a0627aaa8f44e0948b527f8 in the order given by
patches/series.txt.

